WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:11.364
We established that for situations in which you need to create logical networks with devices scattered throughout an infrastructure, you will want to create virtual LANs, VLANs for short.

00:11.364 --> 00:23.792
Let's further plan this scenario. You can create two VLANs on a switch, VLAN1 and VLAN2 for example. You could then allocate half the switch ports to each VLAN.

00:23.792 --> 00:31.049
If, for whatever reason, you end up with more people in operations than in project management, no problem.

00:31.049 --> 00:38.427
You can easily move some physical switch ports to VLAN1 to accommodate the need for more ports being in operations.

00:38.427 --> 00:43.875
That is the beauty of VLANs: being able to adjust the number of ports assigned to each VLAN.

00:43.875 --> 00:55.087
Let's say, as these departments grow, you end up placing a second physical switch in the network. You can then make a connection between this and the original switch a trunk line.

00:55.087 --> 01:01.575
This will extend the VLAN capabilities to the second switch and give you a better physical situation for your network,

01:01.575 --> 01:14.327
as you won't have to run cables over long distances just to connect your devices to a switch. And, on any switch using VLANs, you can allocate ports to each VLAN as needed.

